  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1220 AM CDT Wed Sep 16 2026\n\nAs moisture continues to gradually advect into the region, heat \nindex values will remain high Wednesday with a high to moderate \nrisk of heat-related impacts. Some relief to the heat will come \nlater this week as rain chances continue to look favorable, mainly\nfor Wednesday night and Thursday. As mentioned in the previous \ndiscussion, capped the Max PoP from the NBM to be more in line \nwith the previous forecast due to uncertainty. As models have been\nconsistent with rain chances for Thursday, it's still a little \ncloudy for how widespread they will be as well as the potential \nintensities. While rain chances will be medium to high across the \neastern half of South Texas Thursday, the chances for the western \nhalf will generally remain low with chances less than 40%.\n\nModels show a long easterly fetch developing across the northern \nGulf waters this week which may help push water farther up the \nbeaches. Will continue to monitor trends for slightly higher than \nnormal tides and/or an increased rip current risk.  At this time, \nminor coastal flooding is not anticipated but will continue to be \nmonitored.\n\nMore heat safety information can be found at weather.gov/heat",
